\section{Derivation of Suprakernel Evolution Equations}

\subsection{Unified Suprakernel State Vector}

We begin by defining the global suprakernel state as the concatenation
of all domain-specific states:
\[
\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}} =
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{X}_{\text{flight}} \\
\mathbf{X}_{\text{struct}} \\
\mathbf{X}_{\text{thermal}} \\
\mathbf{X}_{\text{prop}} \\
\mathbf{X}_{\text{ctrl}}
\end{bmatrix}.
\]
Each subvector evolves according to its own governing equations, but the
suprakernel treats $\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}$ as a single organism-like
state subject to a unified constraint envelope.

\subsection{Baseline Evolution Operator}

Let $\mathcal{F}_{\text{SK}}$ denote the baseline evolution operator
over a timestep $\Delta t$:
\[
\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}^{(n+1)} =
\mathcal{F}_{\text{SK}}\big(\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}^{(n)}, \Delta t\big).
\]
In expanded form, this can be written as
\[
\mathcal{F}_{\text{SK}}\big(\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}^{(n)}, \Delta t\big)
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathcal{F}_{\text{flight}} \\
\mathcal{F}_{\text{struct}} \\
\mathcal{F}_{\text{thermal}} \\
\mathcal{F}_{\text{prop}} \\
\mathcal{F}_{\text{ctrl}}
\end{bmatrix}
\big(\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}^{(n)}, \Delta t\big),
\]
where each $\mathcal{F}_{\bullet}$ integrates its respective domain
dynamics over $\Delta t$.

\subsection{Perturbation Damping Derivation}

We introduce a perturbation $\delta \mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}$ arising from
external transients (e.g., aerodynamic shocks or thermal spikes). The
raw perturbation would yield
\[
\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}^{(n+1)} =
\mathcal{F}_{\text{SK}}\big(\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}^{(n)}, \Delta t\big)
+
\delta \mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}.
\]
To enforce stability, we define a damping operator
$\mathcal{D}_{\text{pert}}$ such that
\[
\delta \mathbf{X}'_{\text{SK}} =
\mathcal{D}_{\text{pert}}\big(\delta \mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}\big),
\quad
\|\delta \mathbf{X}'_{\text{SK}}\|
\le
\kappa \|\delta \mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}\|,
\quad 0 < \kappa < 1.
\]
The suprakernel evolution then becomes
\[
\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}^{(n+1)} =
\mathcal{F}_{\text{SK}}\big(\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}^{(n)}, \Delta t\big)
+
\delta \mathbf{X}'_{\text{SK}},
\]
which is a damped perturbation update ensuring bounded deviation from
the baseline trajectory.

\subsection{Thermal Shock Gradient Derivation}

For thermal shock analysis, we define the thermal stiffness matrix
$\mathbf{K}_{\text{th}}$, thermal damping matrix
$\mathbf{C}_{\text{th}}$, and a shock amplification tensor
$\mathbf{S}_{\text{shock}}$. The thermal shock gradient is given by
\[
G_{\text{TS}} =
\delta_{1}\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{K}_{\text{th}})
-
\delta_{2}\lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{C}_{\text{th}})
-
\delta_{3}\lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{S}_{\text{shock}}),
\]
where $\lambda_{\min}$ and $\lambda_{\max}$ denote the extremal
eigenvalues and $\delta_{1}, \delta_{2}, \delta_{3}$ are weighting
coefficients.

The thermal shock margin is then
\[
\text{SM}_{\text{TS}} = G_{\text{TS}} - G_{\text{crit,TS}},
\]
with $G_{\text{crit,TS}}$ representing a critical threshold. The
suprakernel enforces
\[
\text{SM}_{\text{TS}} \ge 0
\]
as a hard constraint for admissible evolution in high-flux regimes.

\subsection{Encrypted Runtime and Ledger Binding}

Each suprakernel state is bound to an encrypted runtime representation:
\[
\Lambda^{(n)} =
\mathcal{E}_{\text{SK}}\big(\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}^{(n)}, k_{\text{sec}}\big),
\]
where $\mathcal{E}_{\text{SK}}$ is an encryption operator and
$k_{\text{sec}}$ is a secret key.

The corresponding ledger entry is
\[
\ell^{(n)} =
\mathcal{L}_{\text{SK}}\big(\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}^{(n)}, \Lambda^{(n)}\big),
\]
and the hash chain is defined recursively as
\[
H^{(n)} =
\text{Hash}\big(\ell^{(n)} \,\|\, H^{(n-1)}\big),
\]
with $H^{(0)}$ initialized by a genesis entry. This construction ensures
immutability and traceability of the entire suprakernel evolution
sequence.

\subsection{Constraint Envelope Formulation}

Finally, the unified constraint envelope is expressed as
\[
\mathcal{C}_{\text{SK}} =
\mathcal{C}_{\text{flight}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{thermal}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{struct}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{prop}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{ctrl}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{enc}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{ledger}}.
\]
The admissible suprakernel evolution is then defined by
\[
\mathbf{X}_{\text{SK}}^{(n+1)} \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{SK}},
\]
which completes the derivation of the suprakernel evolution equations
under multi-domain, encrypted, perturbation-damped constraints.